The two hash functions every hashed container of ca rests on; `ca::hash` (layer 5, `map/`)
picks between them by key type.

- `ca::hash_word(u64)`: one 64x64 to 128-bit multiply folded to 64 bits. Every output bit depends
  on every input bit, so even small sequential integers spread over the whole table. Integers of
  every width hash by value: `5`, `5U` and `ca::u8{5}` agree.
- `ca::hash_bytes(data, size, seed)`: wyhash (final 4). Up to 16 bytes take two or three loads and
  two multiplies with no loop; longer inputs consume 16 bytes per multiply, in three independent
  lanes past 48 bytes so the multiplies overlap.

```c++
#include "ca/hash.hpp"
#include "ca/integers.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const char key[] = "user:42";
	const ca::u64 a = ca::hash_bytes(key, sizeof(key) - 1U);
	const ca::u64 b = ca::hash_bytes(key, sizeof(key) - 1U);
	const ca::u64 seeded = ca::hash_bytes(key, sizeof(key) - 1U, 0x9E37'79B9U);
	if (a != b || a == seeded)
		return 1;
	if (ca::hash_word(5U) != ca::hash_word(ca::u8{5U}))
		return 2;
	return ca::hash_word(1U) != ca::hash_word(2U) ? 0 : 3;
}
```

`hash_word` is constexpr; `hash_bytes` is not, since it reads unaligned words. Both are
deterministic and unseeded: the same input hashes the same in every run and on every machine
of the same byte order. That makes tables reproducible, and also means they offer no defense
against keys chosen to collide; pass a secret `seed` to `hash_bytes` when keys come from an
adversary.
